.TH tail 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
tail \- print the last part of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tail
[\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-c\fI number\fR | \fB\-n\fI number\fR] [\fIfile\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tail
prints the last ten lines of
.IR file ,
or of standard input if none is named.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " number"
Print the last
.I number
lines.  A leading
.B +
counts forward from the start of the file instead.
.TP
.BI \-c " number"
Count bytes rather than lines.
.TP
.B \-f
After reaching the end, keep the file open and print anything more that
is written to it, checking once a second.  Stop it with the interrupt
key.
.SH NOTES
One file only: naming two is a usage error, not a heading followed by
both.  So is giving
.B \-c
and
.B \-n
together.
.PP
When the input is a seekable file,
.B tail
seeks backwards from the end rather than reading the whole thing, so
tailing a large log is cheap.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# tail /var/log/messages
# tail -n 40 prog.c
# tail -f /var/log/messages
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR head (1),
.BR more (1)
